        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
            "DATE_TRUNC the timestamp to month, GROUP BY the truncated month \u2014 time-series analysis needs a consistent time grain before aggregating",
            "GROUP BY the raw timestamp \u2014 each second is a group",
            "Time grain doesn't matter for trends",
            "Use string formatting for months"
          ],
          "evidence": [
            [
              "q.sql",
              "SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', created_at) AS month,\n COUNT(*) AS signups\nFROM users GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1;"
            ]
          ],
          "hint": "What does one row of the result represent \u2014 a second, or a month?",
          "id": "da-date-trunc",
          "mins": 5,
          "objectives": [
            "Time-series aggregation",
            "DATE_TRUNC"
          ],
          "prompt": "How do you build a monthly trend?",
          "situation": "'Signups per month' \u2014 a junior analyst groups by the raw timestamp and gets millions of groups.",
          "title": "The timestamp groups",
          "why": "Time-series analysis starts by choosing the grain: DATE_TRUNC('month', ts) buckets timestamps to month starts, then GROUP BY aggregates. Without truncation, every distinct timestamp is its own group \u2014 noise, not trend. (Syntax varies: DATE_TRUNC in Postgres, DATETRUNC in T-SQL, DATE_FORMAT in MySQL \u2014 the concept is universal.) Also mind time zones: truncate in the business's time zone, not UTC, or month boundaries shift."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
            "COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) \u2014 unique users; COUNT(*) counts events; the ratio is events per user \u2014 know which the question asks",
            "COUNT(*) \u2014 it counts users",
            "They are the same when grouped",
            "COUNT(user_id) counts unique users"
          ],
          "evidence": [
            [
              "q.sql",
              "SELECT DATE_TRUNC('day', ts) AS day,\n COUNT(*) AS events,\n COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS users\nFROM clicks GROUP BY 1;"
            ]
          ],
          "hint": "If one user clicks 50 times, how many 'users' is that \u2014 and how many 'events'?",
          "id": "da-count-distinct-users",
          "mins": 5,
          "objectives": [
            "COUNT DISTINCT",
            "Events vs users"
          ],
          "prompt": "What is the difference, and why does it matter?",
          "situation": "'Daily active users' computed with COUNT(*) shows 10x the known user base \u2014 power users dominate.",
          "title": "The active-user inflation",
          "why": "COUNT(*) counts rows (events); COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) counts unique users. 'Active users' = distinct users; 'engagement' might = events. Mixing them up inflates DAU by the activity of power users. The events-per-user ratio (COUNT(*)/COUNT(DISTINCT)) is itself a useful metric \u2014 but only when you know which numerator and denominator you're using."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
            "Cohort retention: GROUP BY signup month (cohort), then compute % active in each subsequent month \u2014 retention curves reveal whether the product keeps users",
            "Overall monthly actives \u2014 it shows retention",
            "Cohort analysis needs no SQL",
            "Retention equals 1 minus churn always"
          ],
          "evidence": [
            [
              "q.sql",
              "WITH cohorts AS (\n SELECT user_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', signup_at) AS cohort FROM users)\nSELECT c.cohort,\n DATE_TRUNC('month', a.active_at) AS m,\n COUNT(DISTINCT a.user_id) AS active\nFROM cohorts c JOIN activity a ON a.user_id = c.user_id\nGROUP BY 1, 2;"
            ]
          ],
          "hint": "'Users who signed up in January, what % were active in March?' \u2014 what two groupings do you need?",
          "id": "da-cohort",
          "mins": 6,
          "objectives": [
            "Cohort analysis",
            "Retention"
          ],
          "prompt": "How do you measure whether the product retains users?",
          "situation": "Total signups grow, but nobody knows if users stick around. Overall actives look flat.",
          "title": "The leaky bucket",
          "why": "Cohort retention: group users by signup period (the cohort), then track each cohort's activity over subsequent periods \u2014 producing retention curves. Flat overall actives + growing signups = churn masking growth (the leaky bucket). Cohort curves show whether newer cohorts retain better (product improving) or all cohorts decay the same (structural problem). It's the core product-health analysis."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
            "Funnel with step-by-step conversion: count users reaching each stage (ordered), compute conversion between consecutive steps \u2014 drop-off pinpoints friction",
            "Total conversions only \u2014 funnels add nothing",
            "Funnel steps can be in any order",
            "Conversion rates need no denominators"
          ],
          "evidence": [
            [
              "q.sql",
              "WITH steps AS (\n SELECT user_id,\n  MAX(CASE WHEN event='view' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS viewed,\n  MAX(CASE WHEN event='cart' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS carted,\n  MAX(CASE WHEN event='buy' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS bought\n FROM events GROUP BY user_id)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS users,\n SUM(viewed) AS viewed, SUM(carted) AS carted, SUM(bought) AS bought\nFROM steps;"
            ]
          ],
          "hint": "To find where users drop off, what do you need at each stage \u2014 and in what order?",
          "id": "da-funnel",
          "mins": 6,
          "objectives": [
            "Funnel analysis",
            "Conversion"
          ],
          "prompt": "How do you build a conversion funnel in SQL?",
          "situation": "'Where do shoppers drop off?' \u2014 the team has event data but no funnel.",
          "title": "The leaky funnel",
          "why": "Funnel = ordered stages with per-stage user counts; conversion = stage_n / stage_{n-1}. The conditional-aggregation pattern (MAX(CASE WHEN event=...) per user) builds the funnel in one pass. The biggest drop-off between consecutive steps is where friction lives. Caveats: enforce event ordering (cart before buy), define the time window (session? 7 days?), and segment (mobile vs desktop funnels differ wildly)."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
            "Window function: ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY ts DESC) = 1 \u2014 latest row per user without collapsing; or DISTINCT ON / QUALIFY per dialect",
            "GROUP BY user_id with MAX(ts) \u2014 then join back for the full row",
            "ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 1 \u2014 gives one user's latest",
            "Latest row per user is impossible in SQL"
          ],
          "evidence": [
            [
              "q.sql",
              "SELECT * FROM (\n SELECT s.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY ts DESC) AS rn\n FROM subscriptions s) t WHERE rn = 1;"
            ]
          ],
          "hint": "Which pattern keeps the full row of each user's latest event?",
          "id": "da-latest-row",
          "mins": 5,
          "objectives": [
            "Latest record per group",
            "Window functions"
          ],
          "prompt": "How do you get each user's current subscription status?",
          "situation": "A subscriptions table has history rows per user. 'Current status per user' needs the latest row each.",
          "title": "The current status",
          "why": "ROW_NUMBER() partitioned by user ordered by time desc, filter rn=1 \u2014 the standard 'latest per group' pattern, returning full rows. (GROUP BY + MAX gets the timestamp but needs a self-join for the rest of the row.) Dialect shortcuts exist (Postgres DISTINCT ON, Snowflake/BigQuery QUALIFY). Every analyst needs this pattern \u2014 slowly-changing entity state is everywhere."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
            "Percent of total: SUM(amount) / SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER () \u2014 window over the grouped result; or ratio-to-report patterns per dialect",
            "Subquery computing the grand total, joined back \u2014 the only way",
            "Percentages can't be computed in SQL",
            "Divide by COUNT(*) instead"
          ],
          "evidence": [
            [
              "q.sql",
              "SELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total,\n SUM(amount) / SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER () AS pct_of_total\nFROM sales GROUP BY region;"
            ]
          ],
          "hint": "After GROUP BY, what does SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER () compute?",
          "id": "da-pct-of-total",
          "mins": 6,
          "objectives": [
            "Window over aggregates",
            "Percent of total"
          ],
          "prompt": "How do you compute each region's share of total?",
          "situation": "'Each region's % of total sales' \u2014 the analyst writes two queries and divides in a spreadsheet.",
          "title": "The share of total",
          "why": "Window functions evaluate after GROUP BY: SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER () = grand total over the grouped rows, so each row's share = its sum / grand total. One query, no spreadsheet round-trip. (Some dialects need a subquery/CTE: compute grouped sums, then window over them.) It's the 'percent of total' and 'running share' foundation."
        }
      ]
