          "id": "da-pivot",
          "mins": 4,
          "objectives": [
            "Pivot tables",
            "Exploration"
          ],
          "prompt": "When do you reach for a pivot table vs formulas?",
          "situation": "An analyst writes 40 SUMIFS formulas to slice sales by region \u00d7 quarter. The VP asks for 'by product too' \u2014 40 more formulas.",
          "title": "The formula sprawl",
          "why": "Pivot tables are the exploration tool: drag fields to rows/columns/values, slice interactively, no formula maintenance. Formulas (SUMIFS/COUNTIFS/XLOOKUP) suit fixed, repeatable calculations that must be auditable cell-by-cell. Rule: exploring \u2192 pivot; productionizing a fixed report \u2192 formulas (or better, SQL). 40 SUMIFS is a pivot table crying for help."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
            "XLOOKUP (or INDEX+MATCH) \u2014 looks left or right, exact match by default, handles missing gracefully; VLOOKUP only looks right and breaks when columns move",
            "VLOOKUP \u2014 the standard; limitations are fine",
            "VLOOKUP can look left with a flag",
            "Lookup direction doesn't matter"
          ],
          "evidence": [],
          "hint": "The lookup column is to the RIGHT of the return column \u2014 which function handles that?",
          "id": "da-xlookup",
          "mins": 4,
          "objectives": [
            "Lookups",
            "XLOOKUP"
          ],
          "prompt": "Why has XLOOKUP replaced VLOOKUP?",
          "situation": "A VLOOKUP breaks every time someone inserts a column; another needs to look left of the key column.",
          "title": "The broken VLOOKUP",
          "why": "VLOOKUP: searches the first column, returns from a column index to the right \u2014 brittle (column inserts shift the index) and one-directional. XLOOKUP (or INDEX+MATCH): separate lookup and return arrays \u2014 looks any direction, exact match default, built-in if-not-found handling. For analysts still on VLOOKUP: the upgrade removes an entire bug class."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
            "Clean first: TRIM whitespace, standardize case, fix date formats, split/merge columns, remove duplicates \u2014 analysis on dirty data is fiction",
            "Analyze first; cleaning is optional",
            "Dirty data averages out",
            "Whitespace doesn't affect matching"
          ],
          "evidence": [],
          "hint": "'New York' vs 'new york ' vs 'NEW YORK' \u2014 how many distinct values does the pivot see?",
          "id": "da-cleaning",
          "mins": 5,
          "objectives": [
            "Data cleaning",
            "Text normalization"
          ],
          "prompt": "What is the cleaning checklist before analysis?",
          "situation": "A pivot shows 3 versions of 'New York'; dates mix US/EU formats; duplicates inflate counts.",
          "title": "The dirty column",
          "why": "Cleaning checklist: TRIM whitespace, UPPER/LOWER standardization, consistent date parsing (TEXTSPLIT or DATEVALUE with known format), dedupe (Remove Duplicates on key columns), handle blanks vs zeros explicitly. 'New York' \u00d7 3 = 3 groups in the pivot. Garbage in, gospel out is the analyst's original sin \u2014 clean before you conclude."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
            "One row per observation, one column per variable (tidy data); headers in row 1, no merged cells, no totals rows mixed into data \u2014 structure for analysis, format for presentation separately",
            "Merged headers look professional \u2014 keep them",
            "Totals rows inside the data are fine",
            "Multiple tables per sheet is tidiest"
          ],
          "evidence": [],
          "hint": "Can a pivot table read a sheet with merged headers and subtotal rows mixed in?",
          "id": "da-tidy-sheets",
          "mins": 4,
          "objectives": [
            "Tidy data",
            "Spreadsheet structure"
          ],
          "prompt": "What makes a spreadsheet analysis-ready?",
          "situation": "A 'report' sheet with merged headers, subtotal rows, and notes in cells can't be pivoted or imported.",
          "title": "The unpivotable sheet",
          "why": "Tidy structure: row 1 = single-row headers, one observation per row, one variable per column, no merged cells, no embedded totals/notes. Presentation formatting (merges, subtotals) belongs on a separate presentation sheet fed by formulas \u2014 never mixed into the data. If a pivot can't read it, it's not data, it's a picture of data."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
