            "Spreadsheets scale infinitely",
            "Version control works fine with emailed files",
            "1M rows is plenty for any analysis"
          ],
          "evidence": [],
          "hint": "The file is emailed as 'final_v7_REALLY_FINAL.xlsx' \u2014 what broke?",
          "id": "da-sheet-limits",
          "mins": 4,
          "objectives": [
            "Spreadsheet limits",
            "Reproducibility"
          ],
          "prompt": "When do you outgrow spreadsheets?",
          "situation": "A 2M-row 'analysis' spreadsheet crashes; three versions circulate by email; nobody can reproduce last month's numbers.",
          "title": "The final_v7 spreadsheet",
          "why": "Spreadsheet limits: row caps (~1M), no real version control (email chains), manual steps that don't reproduce, silent formula errors at scale. Graduate to SQL (data size, joins), BI tools (scheduled refresh, governed metrics), and version-controlled code when: data exceeds comfortable size, the analysis repeats on a schedule, or more than one person must reproduce it. Spreadsheets are the sketchpad, not the system."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
