          ],
          "prompt": "How can the new design win overall but lose in every segment?",
          "situation": "Overall conversion favors the new design, but it loses within mobile and within desktop separately.",
          "title": "The paradox",
          "why": "Simpson's paradox: a lurking variable (traffic mix) reverses the aggregate. If the new design received mostly high-converting desktop traffic, its overall rate is inflated by mix, not merit. Within each segment, the old design wins. Rule: always segment by known confounders (device, source, region) before trusting an aggregate \u2014 the overall number can lie while every segment tells the truth."
