  "lessons": [
    {
      "domain_id": "sql-analysis",
      "title": "SQL for Analysis \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Time-series analysis starts by choosing the grain: DATE_TRUNC('month', ts) buckets timestamps to month starts, then GROUP BY aggregates.",
        "DATE_TRUNC: (Syntax varies: DATE_TRUNC in Postgres, DATETRUNC in T-SQL, DATE_FORMAT in MySQL \u2014 the concept is universal.) Also mind time zones: truncate in the business's time zone, not UTC, or month boundaries shift.",
        "COUNT(*) counts rows (events); COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) counts unique users. Without truncation, every distinct timestamp is its own group \u2014 noise, not trend.",
        "Events vs users: cohort retention: group users by signup period (the cohort), then track each cohort's activity over subsequent periods \u2014 producing retention curves. 'Active users' = distinct users; 'engagement' might = events.",
        "Cohort curves show whether newer cohorts retain better (product improving) or all cohorts decay the same (structural problem)."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "sql",
        "code": "SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', created_at) AS month,\n COUNT(*) AS signups\nFROM users GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1;",
        "walkthrough": "Time-series analysis starts by choosing the grain: DATE_TRUNC('month', ts) buckets timestamps to month starts, then GROUP BY aggregates. Without truncation, every distinct timestamp is its own group \u2014 noise, not trend."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: cohort analysis needs no SQL.",
        "Common trap: cOUNT(user_id) counts unique users.",
        "Common trap: latest row per user is impossible in SQL.",
        "Common trap: subquery computing the grand total, joined back."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "spreadsheets",
      "title": "Spreadsheets \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Pivot tables are the exploration tool: drag fields to rows/columns/values, slice interactively, no formula maintenance. If a pivot can't read it, it's not data, it's a picture of data.",
        "XLOOKUP (or INDEX+MATCH): separate lookup and return arrays \u2014 looks any direction, exact match default, built-in if-not-found handling. Formulas (SUMIFS/COUNTIFS/XLOOKUP) suit fixed, repeatable calculations that must be auditable cell-by-cell.",
        "Data cleaning: prevent, don't fix: data validation dropdowns constrain categories, date validation constrains formats, conditional formatting flags outliers/duplicates visually, and sheet protection locks formula cells while leaving inputs editable."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Worked example \u2014 pivot tables\nSetup: Pivot tables are the exploration tool: drag fields to rows/columns/values, slice interactively, no formula maintenance.\nReasoning: Rule: exploring \u2192 pivot; productionizing a fixed report \u2192 formulas (or better, SQL).\nTakeaway: Cleaning checklist: TRIM whitespace, UPPER/LOWER standardization, consistent date parsing (TEXTSPLIT or DATEVALUE with known format), dedupe (Remove\u2026",
        "walkthrough": "Read the setup and name the principle at play before looking at the reasoning. Then compare your answer with the takeaway \u2014 if they differ, re-read the concepts above and try the drill scenarios for this domain."
