    {
      "domain_id": "visualization",
      "title": "Visualization \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Honest axes: for segment comparison, small multiples are the most information-dense honest layout.",
        "Chart selection: small multiples: the same chart repeated per segment, identical scales, in a grid \u2014 the eye compares shapes instantly. The chart is an argument \u2014 pick the one that makes the true pattern easiest to see.",
        "Labeling: direct labeling (series names at line ends, values on bars) removes the legend-lookup round trip \u2014 the eye stays on the data.",
        "Legends fail past ~4 similar series.",
        "Color accessibility: rules: colorblind-safe palettes (blue/orange, not red/green), color never the only channel (add labels, shapes, or position), sufficient contrast for text. Then highlight: one accent color draws the eye to the insight; everything else recedes to gray."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Worked example \u2014 honest axes\nSetup: Rules: colorblind-safe palettes (blue/orange, not red/green), color never the only channel (add labels, shapes, or position), sufficient contrast for\u2026\nReasoning: Small multiples: the same chart repeated per segment, identical scales, in a grid \u2014 the eye compares shapes instantly.\nTakeaway: For segment comparison, small multiples are the most information-dense honest layout.",
