    {
      "domain_id": "sql-analysis",
      "title": "Data Analyst \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Aggregate with intent: SUM, COUNT, and AVG answer 'how much' and 'how many', but always check the grain first \u2014 joining before aggregating across a one-to-many relationship silently multiplies rows.",
        "Window functions (ROW_NUMBER, RANK, LAG, SUM ... OVER) compute across rows without collapsing them \u2014 the workhorse for rankings, running totals, and period-over-period change.",
        "Clean before you chart: strip whitespace, fix types deliberately (zip codes are text, not numbers), remove duplicates, and document every transformation so the analysis is reproducible.",
        "Pick the chart for the question: lines for trends over time, bars for comparisons across categories \u2014 and almost never pie, because humans judge position and length far better than angle.",
        "Summarize skew honestly: report the median alongside the mean for skewed distributions, and never average averages that were computed on different denominators."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "sql",
        "code": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT date_trunc('month', order_date) AS m,\n         SUM(amount) AS revenue\n  FROM orders\n  GROUP BY 1\n)\nSELECT m,\n       revenue,\n       revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY m) AS mom_change\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY m;",
        "walkthrough": "The CTE first rolls orders up to one row per month, fixing the grain before any math happens. LAG then reaches back one row in month order to compute month-over-month change \u2014 no self-join needed."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: COUNT(column) skips NULLs while COUNT(*) counts rows \u2014 pick the one you actually mean.",
        "Common trap: spreadsheet auto-conversion on import (leading zeros lost, dates mangled) \u2014 import IDs as text, then convert deliberately.",
        "Common trap: a y-axis that doesn't start at zero, exaggerating tiny differences \u2014 start at zero or annotate the break.",
        "Common trap: presenting correlation as causation in the meeting \u2014 label it 'associated with', not 'drives'."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Labs tab.",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "data-analyst",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/data-analyst"
    },
    {
      "title": "From Static Diagrams to Living Systems",
      "domain_id": "systems-anatomy",
      "concepts": [
        "A textbook diagram shows you one part in isolation \u2014 a living systems view shows you how all parts of Data Analyst move together in sequence",
        "The four-step systems anatomy method: (1) See the whole in context, (2) Cut it open to reveal layers, (3) Walk through the process step-by-step, (4) Pull apart into labeled pieces",
        "Apply this to any system in this course: start with the trigger action, trace through every component it touches, watch the data flow",
        "Interview/exam power: when asked 'how does X work?', narrate the journey \u2014 'it starts here, then...'. This demonstrates senior-level systems thinking"
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "TRIGGER event occurs\n  \u2192 First system receives it (see the handoff)\n  \u2192 Processing happens in sequence (watch each step)\n  \u2192 State changes propagate (follow the ripple)\n  \u2192 Final outcome delivered\n\nEach arrow is a living handoff, not a static box.",
        "walkthrough": "This is the 'jet engine cutaway' for your field: instead of memorizing isolated facts, you see the entire flow as a moving sequence. Practice narrating this journey out loud."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Memorizing terms without understanding the processes they participate in",
        "Drawing architecture diagrams as boxes without showing the data flow between them",
        "Answering 'how does X work?' with a definition instead of walking through the sequence"
      ],
      "try_it": "Pick any process in this course. Write out the full journey from trigger to outcome, naming every system touched."
    },
