  "resources": {
    "cheatsheet": {
      "title": "Data Analyst \u2014 Cheat Sheet",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "SQL for Analysis",
          "items": [
            "Time-series analysis starts by choosing the grain \u2014 DATE_TRUNC('month', ts) buckets timestamps to month starts, then GROUP BY aggregates.",
            "DATE_TRUNC \u2014 (Syntax varies: DATE_TRUNC in Postgres, DATETRUNC in T-SQL, DATE_FORMAT in MySQL \u2014 the concept is universal.) Also mind time zones: truncate in the business's time zone, not UTC, or month boundaries shift.",
            "COUNT \u2014 COUNT(*) counts rows (events); COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) counts unique users. Without truncation, every distinct timestamp is its own group \u2014 noise, not trend.",
            "Events vs users \u2014 cohort retention: group users by signup period (the cohort), then track each cohort's activity over subsequent periods \u2014 producing retention curves. 'Active users' = distinct users; 'engagement' might = events.",
            "Cohort \u2014 Cohort curves show whether newer cohorts retain better (product improving) or all cohorts decay the same (structural problem).",
            "Common trap \u2014 cohort analysis needs no SQL.",
            "Common trap \u2014 cOUNT(user_id) counts unique users.",
            "Common trap \u2014 latest row per user is impossible in SQL.",
            "Common trap \u2014 subquery computing the grand total, joined back.",
            "Time-series analysis starts by choosing the grain \u2014 DATE_TRUNC('month', ts) buckets timestamps to month starts, then GROUP BY aggregates. Without truncation, every distinct timestamp is its own group \u2014 noise, not trend."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Spreadsheets",
          "items": [
            "Pivot tables are the exploration tool \u2014 drag fields to rows/columns/values, slice interactively, no formula maintenance. If a pivot can't read it, it's not data, it's a picture of data.",
            "XLOOKUP (or INDEX+MATCH) \u2014 separate lookup and return arrays \u2014 looks any direction, exact match default, built-in if-not-found handling. Formulas (SUMIFS/COUNTIFS/XLOOKUP) suit fixed, repeatable calculations that must be auditable cell-by-cell.",
            "Data cleaning \u2014 prevent, don't fix: data validation dropdowns constrain categories, date validation constrains formats, conditional formatting flags outliers/duplicates visually, and sheet protection locks formula cells while leaving inputs editable.",
            "Common trap \u2014 pivot tables can't handle large data.",
            "Common trap \u2014 totals rows inside the data are fine.",
            "Common trap \u2014 analyze first; cleaning is optional.",
            "Common trap \u2014 multiple tables per sheet is tidiest.",
            "Read \u2014 Read the setup and name the principle at play before looking at the reasoning. Then compare your answer with the takeaway \u2014 if they differ, re-read the concepts above and try the drill scenarios for this domain."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Visualization",
          "items": [
            "Honest axes \u2014 for segment comparison, small multiples are the most information-dense honest layout.",
            "Chart selection \u2014 small multiples: the same chart repeated per segment, identical scales, in a grid \u2014 the eye compares shapes instantly. The chart is an argument \u2014 pick the one that makes the true pattern easiest to see.",
            "Labeling \u2014 direct labeling (series names at line ends, values on bars) removes the legend-lookup round trip \u2014 the eye stays on the data.",
            "Legends \u2014 Legends fail past ~4 similar series.",
            "Color accessibility \u2014 rules: colorblind-safe palettes (blue/orange, not red/green), color never the only channel (add labels, shapes, or position), sufficient contrast for text. Then highlight: one accent color draws the eye to the insight; everything else recedes to gray.",
            "Common trap \u2014 data-ink ratio is aesthetic trivia.",
            "Common trap \u2014 every chart needs a border and background shading.",
            "Common trap \u2014 axis choice doesn't affect perception.",
            "Common trap \u2014 one chart type fits all data."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Statistics",
          "items": [
            "Skew \u2014 the mean is pulled by skew; the median (50th percentile) resists it.",
            "Correlation vs causation \u2014 before acting on correlation: name plausible confounders, segment (does X help within engagement bands?), and run an experiment.",
            "Confounders \u2014 rule: always segment by known confounders (device, source, region) before trusting an aggregate \u2014 the overall number can lie while every segment tells the truth.",
            "A/B testing \u2014 proper A/B: randomize, pre-register the primary metric and runtime, size for statistical power (enough users to detect a meaningful effect), then read significance at the end.",
            "Common trap \u2014 correlation proves causation with enough data.",
            "Common trap \u2014 A/B tests are unnecessary when correlation is strong.",
            "Common trap \u2014 confounders don't matter with big data.",
            "Common trap \u2014 bias only matters in small samples.",
            "With \u2014 With a 1% base rate, even 99% accuracy yields ~equal true and false positives (10 real lates caught, ~10 false alarms from 990 on-time). The model is fine; the expectation ignored Bayes. Lesson: rare-event prediction needs either much higher precision or a\u2026"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "BI Tools",
          "items": [
            "Semantic layer \u2014 a semantic layer centralizes metric definitions: one governed 'revenue' (with its exact logic, filters, and grain), consumed by all dashboards and self-service. RLS belongs in the semantic layer/platform so it's consistent across every surface.",
            "Metric governance \u2014 dashboards are production artifacts: version their definitions (BI tools with git integration, or exported definitions in version control), document metric logic alongside, require review for certified dashboards, and keep a changelog.",
            "Dashboard performance discipline \u2014 pre-aggregate to the dashboard's grain (materialized/aggregate tables), default to sane date ranges (not 'all time'), ensure filters hit partitioned/indexed columns, and cache.",
            "Aggregates \u2014 a dashboard is a serving layer \u2014 it should query aggregates, not raw fact tables.",
            "Common trap \u2014 dashboards don't need version control.",
            "Common trap \u2014 load raw billions of rows into the dashboard.",
            "Common trap \u2014 users tolerate 2-minute dashboard loads.",
            "Common trap \u2014 security can be handled by telling users not to look."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Storytelling",
          "items": [
            "Pyramid principle \u2014 answer first ('We should do X'), then the 2-3 supporting reasons, then details as backup.",
            "Message titles \u2014 action titles state the insight ('Enterprise churn doubled after onboarding changes'), not the content ('Churn by segment').",
            "Communicating uncertainty \u2014 honest uncertainty: ranges instead of point estimates, key assumptions stated, sensitivity to drivers ('\u00b13 points if renewal rate moves 5%'), and pre-mortem ('what would change this conclusion').",
            "Narrative arc \u2014 context (what's happening), complication (the tension/change), resolution (what we should do).",
            "Common trap \u2014 cute titles: 'Churn analysis'.",
            "Common trap \u2014 titles should describe the chart type.",
            "Common trap \u2014 uncertainty means the analysis failed.",
            "Common trap \u2014 build suspense: background, methodology, then the answer on slide 20."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "glossary": [
      {
        "term": "SQL",
        "def": "Pivot tables are the exploration tool: drag fields to rows/columns/values, slice interactively, no formula maintenance. Formulas (SUMIFS/COUNTIFS/XLOOKUP) suit fixed, repeatable calculations that must be auditable cell-by-cell."
      },
      {
        "term": "GROUP",
        "def": "Window functions evaluate after GROUP BY: SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER () = grand total over the grouped rows, so each row's share = its sum / grand total. One query, no spreadsheet round-trip."
      },
      {
        "term": "GROUP BY",
        "def": "Window functions evaluate after GROUP BY: SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER () = grand total over the grouped rows, so each row's share = its sum / grand total. One query, no spreadsheet round-trip."
      },
      {
        "term": "COUNT",
        "def": "COUNT(*) counts rows (events); COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) counts unique users. 'Active users' = distinct users; 'engagement' might = events."
      },
      {
        "term": "Without",
        "def": "COUNT(*) counts rows (events); COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) counts unique users. Without truncation, every distinct timestamp is its own group \u2014 noise, not trend."
      },
      {
        "term": "MATCH",
        "def": "XLOOKUP (or INDEX+MATCH): separate lookup and return arrays \u2014 looks any direction, exact match default, built-in if-not-found handling. Formulas (SUMIFS/COUNTIFS/XLOOKUP) suit fixed, repeatable calculations that must be auditable cell-by\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Who",
        "def": "Who decides what 'active user' means \u2014 each dashboard author, or one governed definition?"
      },
      {
        "term": "Dashboards",
        "def": "Dashboards are production artifacts: version their definitions (BI tools with git integration, or exported definitions in version control), document metric logic alongside, require review for certified dashboards, and keep a changelog. '\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "SUMIFS",
        "def": "Pivot tables are the exploration tool: drag fields to rows/columns/values, slice interactively, no formula maintenance. Formulas (SUMIFS/COUNTIFS/XLOOKUP) suit fixed, repeatable calculations that must be auditable cell-by-cell."
      },
      {
        "term": "Time-series",
        "def": "Time-series analysis starts by choosing the grain: DATE_TRUNC('month', ts) buckets timestamps to month starts, then GROUP BY aggregates."
      },
      {
        "term": "A/B",
        "def": "A/B testing: proper A/B: randomize, pre-register the primary metric and runtime, size for statistical power (enough users to detect a meaningful effect), then read significance at the end."
      },
      {
        "term": "Pivot",
        "def": "Pivot tables are the exploration tool: drag fields to rows/columns/values, slice interactively, no formula maintenance. If a pivot can't read it, it's not data, it's a picture of data."
      },
      {
        "term": "Sales",
        "def": "'Sales are flat' (context), 'but enterprise is collapsing while SMB grows' (tension) \u2014 what's the resolution?"
      },
      {
        "term": "Honest",
        "def": "Honest axes: for segment comparison, small multiples are the most information-dense honest layout."
      },
      {
        "term": "VLOOKUP",
        "def": "VLOOKUP: searches the first column, returns from a column index to the right \u2014 brittle (column inserts shift the index) and one-directional. XLOOKUP (or INDEX+MATCH): separate lookup and return arrays \u2014 looks any direction, exact match d\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "DISTINCT",
        "def": "COUNT(*) counts rows (events); COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) counts unique users. Without truncation, every distinct timestamp is its own group \u2014 noise, not trend."
      },
      {
        "term": "Labeling",
        "def": "Labeling: direct labeling (series names at line ends, values on bars) removes the legend-lookup round trip \u2014 the eye stays on the data."
      },
      {
        "term": "Dashboard",
        "def": "Dashboard performance discipline: pre-aggregate to the dashboard's grain (materialized/aggregate tables), default to sane date ranges (not 'all time'), ensure filters hit partitioned/indexed columns, and cache. A dashboard is a serving l\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Correlation",
        "def": "Correlation: X-users retain more. Causation would need: X causes retention."
      },
      {
        "term": "CTE",
        "def": "Window functions evaluate after GROUP BY: SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER () = grand total over the grouped rows, so each row's share = its sum / grand total. One query, no spreadsheet round-trip."
      },
      {
        "term": "Rule",
        "def": "Pivot tables are the exploration tool: drag fields to rows/columns/values, slice interactively, no formula maintenance. Formulas (SUMIFS/COUNTIFS/XLOOKUP) suit fixed, repeatable calculations that must be auditable cell-by-cell."
      },
      {
        "term": "T-SQL",
        "def": "DATE_TRUNC: (Syntax varies: DATE_TRUNC in Postgres, DATETRUNC in T-SQL, DATE_FORMAT in MySQL \u2014 the concept is universal.) Also mind time zones: truncate in the business's time zone, not UTC, or month boundaries shift."
      },
      {
        "term": "MySQL",
        "def": "DATE_TRUNC: (Syntax varies: DATE_TRUNC in Postgres, DATETRUNC in T-SQL, DATE_FORMAT in MySQL \u2014 the concept is universal.) Also mind time zones: truncate in the business's time zone, not UTC, or month boundaries shift."
      },
      {
